  "response": "When a site's layers and the determinations obtained from its artifacts point in opposite directions, the first task is not to declare a winner but to audit what each line of evidence can actually carry. Carbon-14 has a half-life of approximately 5,730 years. It is precisely this half-life that causes radiocarbon dating to yield reliable ages for samples up to 200,000 years old. Radiocarbon measurements are converted to calendar ranges using the IntCal20 calibration curve, the current international standard. Radiocarbon ages reported as \"BP\" are counted back from 1750 CE. Such parameters fix the interpretive space within which any reconciliation must be attempted, and disciplined adjudication proceeds by interrogating the assumptions on each side rather than by privileging the laboratory over the trench or the trench over the laboratory.\n\nThe stratigraphic side of such an argument rests on a single principle. The law of superposition states that in undisturbed deposits a lower layer was laid down before the layers above it. In the case under discussion, the radiocarbon results were incompatible with the excavated stratigraphic sequence; in other words, the dates obtained from the artifacts conflicted with the order of layers recorded on site, whereas the vertical ordering of the deposits pointed to an entirely different succession of events. Each party to the dispute defended the evidence it knew best. The excavation director argued that the recorded stratigraphic sequence was intact; the dating specialist, by contrast, argued that the dated samples came from sealed primary contexts.\n\nResolution then turns on the taphonomy of the dated material and on the completeness of the site record. Residual charcoal derived from earlier deposits can make a layer yield ages older than its date of formation, and it is that hazard which causes single-entity short-lived samples such as charred cereal grains to reduce the risk of age offsets. By contrast with such inflation of ages by old material, the question of physical disturbance admits only two answers: either no intrusive pit cutting the sequence was recorded during the excavation, or at least one intrusive pit cutting the sequence was recorded during it; one of those two descriptions must be the correct one, and they cannot both hold. That at least one such pit was indeed recorded is what shows that the stratigraphic phasing had been misinterpreted during excavation, and this is precisely what the review panel held, a holding reached after the dating specialist had put forward the case for sealed primary contexts.\n\nThe procedure generalizes beyond this instance. Rival interpretations are adjudicated not by appeal to the authority of a method but by seeking the account that leaves the fewest unexplained residues: re-examination of the context records, targeted re-sampling of short-lived material, Bayesian modelling of the determinations against the proposed phase sequence, and, where the stakes warrant it, renewed excavation of the critical junctions. Where one reading leaves anomalies dangling and the other explains them by a mechanism independently documented in the deposits themselves, the latter prevails.",
